# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored — never commit it.
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. Substitute your own; commit nothing real.

# ===========================================================================
# DATABASE
#
# Two variables, always, in every environment:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  what the running app connects with
#   DIRECT_URL    what migrations and CLI scripts connect with
#
# They are kept separate even when they hold the same string, because in
# production they must NOT be the same — see "Hosted Postgres" below. Code
# reads them by name, so the difference between local and prod stays entirely
# in this file.
#
# If your password contains reserved URL characters (@ : / ? # [ ] %) they
# must be percent-encoded — an @ becomes %40, or the URL parser will split on
# the wrong character and the host will be wrong.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- Local development: one Postgres, no pooler ---------------------------
# There is no PgBouncer in front of a local server, so both strings are the
# same and DB_POOLED stays false.
#
# 5432 is the Postgres default, but check it is actually free before using it
# — another project's container or a system install may already hold it, and
# connecting to the wrong server looks like a password failure against yours.
# Any free port works; see README → Local Postgres.

DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:PASSWORD@localhost:5434/cico"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:PASSWORD@localhost:5434/cico"

# ---- Hosted Postgres (production) -----------------------------------------
# Supabase gives you two connection strings and they are NOT interchangeable:
#
#   pooled  port 6543, aws-<n>-<region>.pooler.supabase.com
#           DATABASE_URL. Serverless functions open a connection per
#           invocation and would exhaust the direct connection limit.
#
#   direct  port 5432, db.<ref>.supabase.co
#           DIRECT_URL. PgBouncer in transaction mode cannot run some DDL,
#           so migrations must not go through the pooler.
#
# Find both under: Supabase → Project Settings → Database.
#
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT_REF:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres"
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:PASSWORD@db.PROJECT_REF.supabase.co:5432/postgres"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DB_POOLED
#
# "true" only when DATABASE_URL points at a transaction-mode pooler, which
# cannot support prepared statements — a statement prepared on one connection
# is gone by the time the next transaction lands on another.
#
# Set explicitly rather than sniffed from the port: self-hosted PgBouncer
# often listens on 5432, so a port match would disable prepared statements on
# a direct connection that merely looked pooled — or leave them on against a
# real pooler, where the failure only shows up under load.
#
# Local (direct connection):  false, or simply unset.
# Production (6543 pooler):   true.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DB_POOLED="false"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SITE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute origin, used for Open Graph and Twitter card URLs.
# Defaults to http://localhost:3000 when unset.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://cashincashout.vercel.app"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERCEL BLOB - product image uploads
#
# A read-write credential for the ENTIRE blob store, so it is server-only:
# lib/storage/index.ts is guarded with `server-only` to keep it out of the
# browser bundle. Never expose it through a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable.
#
# Get one: Vercel -> Storage -> your Blob store -> .env.local tab, or run
# `vercel env pull .env.local` once the store is linked to the project.
# Tokens look like: vercel_blob_rw_<storeId>_<random>
#
# Verify it works before building any UI:
#   npx tsx --env-file=.env.local scripts/blob-check.ts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN="vercel_blob_rw_STOREID_RANDOMSTRING"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CUSTOMER SIGN-IN (email one-time codes)
#
# OTP_SECRET IS REQUIRED for customer sign-in. It is the HMAC key that
# lib/auth/otp.ts uses to hash one-time codes and session tokens, so the
# database never holds either in a usable form.
#
# There is no fallback and that is deliberate. A random per-process default
# would differ between serverless instances, so a code issued by one would
# fail verification on another — intermittently and unreproducibly. Unset,
# sign-in refuses cleanly instead.
#
# CHANGING IT invalidates every outstanding code and every customer session
# at once. That is the emergency lever if it ever leaks.
#
# Generate one:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# Verify the whole flow:
#   npx tsx --env-file=.env.local scripts/otp-check.ts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

OTP_SECRET="replace-me-with-32-random-bytes-hex"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CUSTOMER_SESSION_SECRET — signs customer session cookies.
#
# A SEPARATE KEY FROM EVERYTHING ELSE, on purpose. Distinct secrets mean a
# leak is containable: rotating this signs every customer out without touching
# admin access or invalidating outstanding one-time codes, and rotating either
# of those does not sign customers out. One shared key would turn any single
# compromise into all three.
#
# Never set it to the same value as OTP_SECRET or ADMIN_PASSWORD.
#
# Required, with no fallback, for the same reason OTP_SECRET is — a random
# per-process default would differ between serverless instances, so a session
# created by one would be rejected by another.
#
# Verify sessions and the admin separation guarantee:
#   npx tsx --env-file=.env.local scripts/session-check.ts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

CUSTOMER_SESSION_SECRET="replace-me-with-different-32-random-bytes-hex"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SENDING EMAIL (SMTP)
#
# Plain SMTP, no provider SDK — the sending service is these variables, not a
# dependency. Gmail locally, SES or Brevo in production, anything else later,
# with no code change.
#
# SMTP_HOST IS THE SWITCH. Leave it unset in development and one-time codes
# are printed to the server console instead of sent. That fallback requires
# NODE_ENV=development and is impossible to reach in production, where a
# missing relay fails outright rather than logging live codes.
#
# SMTP_PORT   587 with STARTTLS is the usual choice; 465 is implicit TLS.
# SMTP_SECURE true only for 465. Defaults from the port when unset.
# EMAIL_FROM  Must be an address the relay is allowed to send as, or it will
#             be rejected or land in spam. See README -> Sending email.
#
# Gmail needs an APP PASSWORD, not your account password — 2FA must be on.
# https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SMTP_HOST="smtp.gmail.com"
# SMTP_PORT="587"
# SMTP_SECURE="false"
# SMTP_USER="you@gmail.com"
# SMTP_PASS="app-password-with-no-spaces"
# EMAIL_FROM="CICO <you@gmail.com>"

# Overrides the hosted wordmark used in email templates. Rarely needed.
# EMAIL_LOGO_URL="https://<store>.public.blob.vercel-storage.com/brand/cico-wordmark.png"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN (/admin)
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD IS REQUIRED. With it unset, /admin refuses every sign-in:
# the fallback in lib/admin-auth.ts is random bytes generated per process
# that nobody can reproduce or type. That is deliberate — it fails CLOSED.
#
# This used to have a committed default, which meant a deployment that forgot
# the variable silently ran on a password published in the repository. THAT
# OLD PASSWORD IS COMPROMISED: it is permanently in git history and must
# never be reused here or anywhere else. See README -> Admin.
#
# A sign-in is always required, in every environment including local
# development. Unauthenticated requests redirect to /admin/login.
#
# Use a long random string. Generate one:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('base64url'))"
#
# ADMIN_EMAIL is the identity checked alongside it, case-insensitively. It is
# not a secret and keeps a sensible default.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

ADMIN_PASSWORD="replace-me-with-a-long-random-string"
# ADMIN_EMAIL="you@example.com"
